<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Massufa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Massufa were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a> and among the early supporters of the Almoravid movement in the 11th century. They controlled much of the caravan traffic across the desert, including the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a> and the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walata">Walata</a>. The 14th-century traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> crossed the Sahara with Massufa guides and described their customs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/massufa.jpg" alt="Massufa" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kabz15 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanhaja_tribes_11th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
